{:edb/db-before-t 1394 :edb/db-after-t 1395 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534707 50 #inst "2026-10-08T04:41:44.029Z" 13194139534707 true] [17592186124213 1100 "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\in V$. Given that $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\{4\\mathbf{v}_1, 5\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly independent\n2. $\\{\\mathbf{0}, \\mathbf{v}_1, 2\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ is linearly independent\n3. $\\{\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent" 13194139534707 false] [17592186124213 1100 "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\in V.$ Given that $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\{ 4\\mathbf{v}_1, 5\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly independent\n\n- $\\{ \\mathbf{0}, \\mathbf{v}_1, 2\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ is linearly independent\n\n- $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent" 13194139534707 true] [17592186124213 1145 "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(4{v}_{1})+{x}_{2}(5{v}_{2})+{x}_{3}{v}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{x}_{1}){v}_{1}+(5{x}_{2}){v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is $x_1=x_2=x_3=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n\\{ 4\\mathbf{v}_1, 5\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + 0 \\cdot (2\\mathbf{v}_2) = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, $x_1\\mathbf{0}+x_2\\mathbf{v}_1+x_3(2\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{0}$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{ \\mathbf{0}, \\mathbf{v}_1, 2\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $x_1\\mathbf{v}_2+x_2\\mathbf{v}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ must have only the zero solution $x_1=x_2=0.$  If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + b \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_3=\\mathbf{0},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + a \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + b \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534707 true] [17592186124213 1145 "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n$x_{1}(4v_{1}) + x_{2}(5v_{2}) + x_{3}v_{3}|= 0$,\nwhich, in turn, gives\n$(4x_{1})v_{1} + (5x_{2})v_{2} + x_{3}v_{3}|= 0$.\nSince $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n${4x_{1} = 0; 5x_{2} = 0; x_{3} = 0$.\nThe unique solution of the system is $x_1=x_2=x_3=0$, which means that $\\{4\\mathbf{v}_1, 5\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly independent.\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, notice that\n$1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + 0 \\cdot (2\\mathbf{v}_2) = \\mathbf{0}$.\nAs a result, $x_1\\mathbf{0}+x_2\\mathbf{v}_1+x_3(2\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{0}$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{\\mathbf{0}, \\mathbf{v}_1, 2\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $x_1\\mathbf{v}_2+x_2\\mathbf{v}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ must have only the zero solution $x_1=x_2=0$. If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + b \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_3=\\mathbf{0}$, then we obtain\n$0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + a \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + b \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$,\nwhich would mean that $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534707 false] [17592186124214 1100 "Consider the set of matrices $\\big\\{A, B \\big\\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\dfrac{x_1}{x_2}$ from a non-zero solution of $x_1A+x_2B=O$, where $O$ is the $2 \\times 2$ zero-matrix." 13194139534707 false] [17592186124214 1100 "Consider the set of matrices $\\big\\{A, B \\big\\},$ where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\dfrac{x_1}{x_2}$ from a non-zero solution of $x_1A+x_2B=O,$ where $O$ is the $2 \\times 2$ zero-matrix." 13194139534707 true] [17592186124214 1145 "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist $x_1,x_2$ not both zero such that\n\n$$\nx_1A+x_2B=O\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_2 = 0\n$$\n\nthe set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the matrices $A$ and $B$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & 0 \\\\ -2x_{1} & 4x_{1}\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{2} & 5x_{2} \\\\ 2x_{2} & -6x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} + 3x_{2} & 5x_{2} \\\\ -2x_{1} + 2x_{2} & 4x_{1} - 6x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 3x_{2} = 0; 5x_{2} = 0; - 2x_{1} + 2x_{2} = 0; 4x_{1} - 6x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + 2R_{1} \\\\ R_{4}:=R_{4} + (-4)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -18 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-\\frac{8}{5})R_{2} \\\\\n&= R_{4}:=R_{4} + \\frac{18}{5}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. Therefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 3x_{2} = 0; 5x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nwhich has only the zero solution $x_1=x_2=0$.\n\nConsequently, $\\big\\{A, B \\big\\}$ is linearly independent." 13194139534707 false] [17592186124214 1145 "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist $x_1,x_2$ not both zero such that \n\n$$\nx_1A+x_2B=O.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if $x_1 = x_2 = 0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the matrices $A$ and $B$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & 0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1} & 4{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} & 5{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} & -6{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & 5{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & 4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-4){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -18 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{8}{5}){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+\\frac{18}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. Therefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has only the zero solution $x_1=x_2=0.$\n\nConsequently, $\\big\\{A, B \\big\\}$ is linearly independent." 13194139534707 true] [17592186124215 1100 "Consider the set $\\big\\{ p_1(t), p_2(t),p_3(t) \\big\\}$ of polynomials from $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ where\n\n$$\np_1(t)=1+t, \\qquad \np_2(t)=-2t-4, \\qquad\np_3(t)=-2t-3.\n$$\n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\dfrac{x_2}{x_1}$ from a non-zero solution of $x_1 \\cdot p_1(t)+x_2 \\cdot p_2(t) + x_3 \\cdot p_3(t)=0.$" 13194139534707 true] [17592186124215 1100 "Consider the set $\\big\\{p_1(t), p_2(t),p_3(t) \\big\\}$ of polynomials from $\\mathbb{R}[t]$, where\n$p_1(t)=1+t, \\qquad p_2(t)=-2t-4, \\qquad p_3(t)=-2t-3$.\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\dfrac{x_2}{x_1}$ from a non-zero solution of $x_1 \\cdot p_1(t)+x_2 \\cdot p_2(t) + x_3 \\cdot p_3(t)=0$." 13194139534707 false] [17592186124215 1145 "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist $x_1,x_2,x_3$ not all zero such that\n\n$$\nx_1p_1(t)+x_2p_2(t)+x_3p_3(t)=0\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0\n$$\n\nthe set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for $p_1(t),p_2(t)$, and $p_3(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(1 + t) + x_{2}(-2t - 4) + x_{3}(-2t - 3) &= 0 \\\\\n(x_{1} - 4x_{2} - 3x_{3}) + (x_{1} - 2x_{2} - 2x_{3})t &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if\n\n$$\n{x_{1} - 4x_{2} - 3x_{3} = 0; x_{1} - 2x_{2} - 2x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, $x_3$ is a free variable. The system is now\n\n$$\n{x_{1} - 4x_{2} - 3x_{3} = 0; 2x_{2} + x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2=-\\dfrac 12x_3\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1-4\\left(-\\dfrac 12x_3\\right)-3x_3=0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow \\qquad x_1=x_3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nFinally, for any\n\n$$\nx_3 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x_2}{x_1}=\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 12x_3\\right)}{x_3}=-\\dfrac 12\n$$" 13194139534707 false] [17592186124215 1145 "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist $x_1,x_2,x_3$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\nx_1p_1(t)+x_2p_2(t)+x_3p_3(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for $p_1(t),p_2(t),$ and $p_3(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(1+t)+{x}_{2}(-2t-4)+{x}_{3}(-2t-3) & =0 \\\\ ({x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3})+({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, $x_3$ is a free variable. The system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=-\\dfrac 12x_3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1-4\\left(-\\dfrac 12x_3\\right)-3x_3=0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow \\qquad x_1=x_3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, for any $x_3 \\neq 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x_2}{x_1}=\\dfrac{\\left( -\\dfrac 12x_3\\right)}{x_3}=-\\dfrac 12.\n$$" 13194139534707 true] [17592186124403 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304607 13194139534707 true] [17592186124403 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304614 13194139534707 true] [17592186124403 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304628 13194139534707 true] [17592186124403 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304642 13194139534707 true] [17592186124404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304600 13194139534707 true] [17592186124404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304635 13194139534707 true] [17592186124404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304663 13194139534707 true] [17592186124404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304670 13194139534707 true] [17592186124404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304677 13194139534707 true] [17592186124405 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304586 13194139534707 true] [17592186124405 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304593 13194139534707 true] [17592186124405 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304621 13194139534707 true] [17592186124405 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304649 13194139534707 true] [17592186124405 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304656 13194139534707 true] [17592186124815 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534707 true] [17592186124815 1100 "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors $v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}, v_{4} ∈ V$. Given that ${v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}}$ is linearly independent and $v_{4} = 3v_{1} - 2v_{2} + 5v_{3}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $v_{2}$ is a linear combination of $v_{1}$, $v_{3}$, and $v_{4}$\n2. $x_{1}v_{1} + x_{2}v_{2} + x_{3}v_{3} + x_{4}v_{4} = 0$ has only zero solution $x_{1} = x_{2} = x_{3} = x_{4} = 0$\n3. ${v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}, v_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set" 13194139534707 false] [17592186124815 1100 "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent and ${v}_{4}=3{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+5{v}_{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{3},$ and ${v}_{4}$\n- ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has only zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0$\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set" 13194139534707 true] [17592186124815 1145 "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, solving equation ${v}_{4}=3{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+5{v}_{3}$ for ${v}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{v}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{v}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{3},$ and ${v}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${v}_{4}=3{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+5{v}_{3},$ we have that\n\n$$\n3\\cdot {v}_{1}-2\\cdot {v}_{2}+5\\cdot {v}_{3}+(-1)\\cdot {v}_{4}=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has a non-zero solution.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has a non-zero solution, the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set by definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534707 true] [17592186304586 1017 #edb/ref 17592186304587 13194139534707 true] [17592186304586 1089 "q-107020" 13194139534707 true] [17592186304586 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534707 true] [17592186304586 1100 "Consider the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ of polynomials from $R[t],$ where\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-2+2t+2{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1-4{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{3}(t)=-2+3t-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{3}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}\\cdot {p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}\\cdot {p}_{3}(t)=0.$" 13194139534707 true] [17592186304586 1113 #uuid "70c35fee-dc82-5545-80b6-389daf251aa5" 13194139534707 true] [17592186304586 1145 "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),$ and ${p}_{3}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-2+2t+2{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(1-4{t}^{2})+{x}_{3}(-2+3t-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (-2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})+(2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3})t+(2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.\n\nThe system is now\n\n