 ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}+{v}_{3},2{v}_{3}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${x}_{1}{v}_{2}+{x}_{2}{v}_{3}=0$ must have only the zero solution ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$  If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {v}_{2}+b\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n0\\cdot {v}_{1}+a\\cdot {v}_{2}+b\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {v}_{1}+1\\cdot {v}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot ({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534707 true] [17592186304608 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304609 13194139534707 true] [17592186304608 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304610 13194139534707 true] [17592186304608 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304611 13194139534707 true] [17592186304608 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304612 13194139534707 true] [17592186304608 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304613 13194139534707 true] [17592186304608 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534707 true] [17592186304608 1041 "selection" 13194139534707 true] [17592186304608 1104 #edb/ref 17592186304612 13194139534707 true] [17592186304608 1110 #uuid "ba2e8c1c-d628-5743-8d86-c18a87fd2bfa" 13194139534707 true] [17592186304609 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534707 true] [17592186304609 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534707 true] [17592186304609 1111 #uuid "ec04b5d0-1115-5135-a55c-7386bf8b0701" 13194139534707 true] [175921863046